WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.370
STEWART BRAND: So on the one hand, you've got the sort of top of the investment level

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:11.460
perspective on art: this incredibly
expensive and rather magnificent museum

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:19.859
going in. I was an artist in the Lower East Side of
New York back in the day and the people

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:23.490
I was hanging out were of course on the
Lower East Side because that's where it was

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:32.040
cheap to live. And everything was on the
"just getting by" level. And so I assume

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.500
that's going on with many young ambitious
artists in Hong Kong and elsewhere. And

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.920
then that overlaps into, in a sense, art is
always rebellion, anyway. You're always

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.379
rebelling against the standard art of
before, you're rebelling against "The Man,"

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:58.469
whoever that institution is seen as. And protesters I know in the United States are in awe of

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:04.080
the protesters in Hong Kong right now,
who seem to have more creative

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:12.540
techniques, and more creative artistry in
a way in their protests. That's pretty

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.799
interesting and bottom-up kind of
creativity. What's your perspective on that?

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:23.310
SUHANYA RAFFEL: It's very interesting to watch the
the visual language that's being applied

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:30.409
now in Hong Kong. Primarily, of course,
it's a tool for communication; it has

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:35.640
nothing to do with the museum per se. But as
a museum, we are very interested in that

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:45.270
as a visual culture form. The protesters
use the this emblem to describe

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:50.430
themselves: they talk about
themselves as being like water. So they

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:59.130
will flow. They're free-flowing. They're
leaderless. They're spontaneous. And so a

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:03.840
lot of the material
that's been that's taking place in terms

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.959
of protest is digital. And it's a
digital form as a communication tool.

00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:17.420
And all of it is unauthored at this point. They do not want to have a single author.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:23.000
It's not in their interest to do so. Our teams are very interested, we're out

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:28.700
documenting. We have archives. And so we
can document, we are doing that. And

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:35.849
researching. How or when and if it enters
the collection, it has to go through the

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:46.110
usual museum mechanism and process of
thinking and looking and seeking rights:

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:51.690
Who owns these things? And perhaps they
are not to be owned, and that's fine. But

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:56.940
then it exists in other kinds of ways. So
it's a very very interesting

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:04.290
phenomena this time. Unlike say, much, much
earlier periods of protest art, which had

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:12.329
very clear activist expressions. In the U.S. there

00:03:12.329 --> 00:03:16.349
are the Guerrilla Girls and there are
whole portfolios of prints and so on

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:20.459
that are very clearly authored and
there for distribution. This is a very

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:28.709
different mechanism that that's taking
place in Hong Kong. And the digital tool

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:32.840
is phenomenal.

